---
// Cyber Kill Chain Reference — Lockheed Martin's 7-phase intrusion model.
// A Frameworks-category sibling of attack-map.astro/d3fend/index.astro/
// threat-actors.astro, but the dataset itself is small and fixed (7 phases,
// not a large generated catalog), so — same call osi-model.astro/
// network-ports.astro already made for their own fixed reference data — it's
// inlined directly below rather than split into src/data/, and rendered as a
// single linear "spine" (no TagCombobox/ListFilter; nothing here is filtered
// or searched, there's just one canonical reading order).
//
// Every phase name and definition is checked directly against the primary
// source: Hutchins, Cloppert & Amin, "Intelligence-Driven Computer Network
// Defense Informed by Analysis of Adversary Campaigns and Intrusion Kill
// Chains" (Lockheed Martin, 2011) — see EXTERNAL_RESOURCES['kill-chain']. Each
// phase's "what defenders look for" line is drawn from that same paper's own
// Courses of Action Matrix (Table 1: detect/deny/disrupt/degrade/deceive/
// destroy per phase), described in plain DFIR terms rather than re-printing
// the paper's own table. The ATT&CK cross-links are a hand-checked mapping,
// not a generated one — Weaponization and Actions on Objectives are the two
// phases that do NOT map cleanly onto a single ATT&CK tactic, and say so
// explicitly (via `attackCaveat`) rather than forcing a fake 1:1, per this
// site's own "never force a mapping that doesn't exist" rule.
export const prerender = true;
import { css } from 'styled-system/css';
import { card } from 'styled-system/recipes';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import type { IconName } from '../components/Icon.astro';
import Term from '../components/Term.astro';
import ExternalResources from '../components/ExternalResources.astro';
import { EXTERNAL_RESOURCES } from '../data/externalResources';
import { toolChip } from '../components/RelatedTools.astro';
import { tagSlug } from '../utils/posts';
import { ATTACK_TACTIC_ORDER } from '../data/attack-techniques.generated';

interface AttackTactic {
  name: string;
  id: string;
}
interface KillChainPhase {
  num: number;
  name: string;
  icon: IconName;
  gist: string;
  definition: string;
  detect: string;
  /** Tactics that genuinely correspond — omitted (not forced) where none cleanly does. */
  attackTactics: AttackTactic[];
  /** Explains a partial/approximate match, or the absence of one. Always shown when set. */
  attackCaveat: string;
}

const KILL_CHAIN_PHASES: KillChainPhase[] = [
  {
    num: 1,
    name: 'Reconnaissance',
    icon: 'search',
    gist: 'Research, identify, and select targets before ever touching them.',
    definition:
      "Researching, identifying, and selecting targets. Lockheed Martin's own paper describes this as often looking like crawling public sources — conference proceedings, mailing lists, company websites — to harvest employee email addresses, social relationships, or details about the specific technologies a target runs.",
    detect:
      "Most of this happens against information that's already public, so a defender's visibility is limited. Lockheed Martin's own Courses of Action Matrix still names web analytics — unusual crawling patterns, or repeated automated hits against public-facing pages and staff directories — as the primary detection option here, and a firewall access-control list blocking known-hostile source ranges as the primary way to deny it outright.",
    attackTactics: [{ name: 'Reconnaissance', id: 'TA0043' }],
    attackCaveat:
      "About as clean a match as this page has — same name on both sides, and MITRE's own tactic definition (gathering information to plan future operations) is nearly interchangeable with Lockheed Martin's.",
  },
  {
    num: 2,
    name: 'Weaponization',
    icon: 'wrench',
    gist: 'Coupling a remote access trojan with an exploit into one deliverable payload.',
    definition:
      'Fusing an exploit and a remote-access payload into one deliverable, usually produced by an automated "weaponizer" tool. The paper notes this deliverable increasingly rides inside an everyday client file — a PDF or an Office document — rather than a standalone executable.',
    detect:
      "Weaponization happens almost entirely off the target's own network, so a defender rarely observes it directly. What the Courses of Action Matrix lists instead are controls that catch the resulting artifact once it starts moving — network intrusion detection and prevention systems flagging a known-bad payload signature in transit.",
    attackTactics: [{ name: 'Resource Development', id: 'TA0042' }],
    attackCaveat:
      "ATT&CK has no dedicated \"weaponization\" tactic. The closest conceptual match, Resource Development, is broader than weaponization alone — it also covers acquiring infrastructure, accounts, and services that have nothing to do with building a payload. This is the clearest case on this page of a phase that doesn't cleanly map onto a single tactic.",
  },
  {
    num: 3,
    name: 'Delivery',
    icon: 'mail',
    gist: 'Getting the weapon to the target — overwhelmingly by email, web, or removable media.',
    definition:
      "Transmitting the weapon to the target environment. Lockheed Martin's own incident response team reported that, across 2004–2010, the three most common delivery vectors for weaponized payloads were email attachments, malicious or compromised websites, and USB removable media.",
    detect:
      "The Courses of Action Matrix lists a trained, suspicious end user reporting a phishing attempt as this phase's own detection control, alongside a web proxy filter to deny malicious sites, inline antivirus scanning of inbound content to disrupt it in transit, and deliberately queuing attachments to buy time for scanning as a way to degrade the delivery.",
    attackTactics: [{ name: 'Initial Access', id: 'TA0001' }],
    attackCaveat:
      "One of the cleaner matches here — the three vectors above map closely onto specific Initial Access techniques (Phishing for email attachments, Drive-by Compromise for malicious websites, Replication Through Removable Media for USB).",
  },
  {
    num: 4,
    name: 'Exploitation',
    icon: 'bug',
    gist: "The delivered weapon triggers the intruder's code on the victim host.",
    definition:
      "After delivery, exploitation triggers the intruder's code on the victim host — most often by targeting a vulnerability in an application or the operating system, though the paper is explicit that it can just as easily exploit the user directly or abuse an OS feature that auto-executes code, with no vulnerability involved at all.",
    detect:
      "Host intrusion detection is the paper's own primary example here; patching (denying the exploit its vulnerability before an attempt ever arrives) and OS-level exploit mitigations like data execution prevention are listed as ways to deny or disrupt it outright.",
    attackTactics: [{ name: 'Execution', id: 'TA0002' }],
    attackCaveat:
      "Execution is the closest match — MITRE frames the tactic as running malicious code, nearly a restatement of the phase's own definition — but MITRE doesn't classify every exploitation technique there. Exploit Public-Facing Application and Drive-by Compromise's own exploit step both sit under Initial Access instead, since ATT&CK groups a technique by the tactical goal it serves rather than by mechanism. Exploitation as a mechanism really spans two tactics, not one.",
  },
  {
    num: 5,
    name: 'Installation',
    icon: 'download',
    gist: 'Installing a backdoor so the foothold survives a reboot.',
    definition:
      "Installing a remote access trojan or backdoor on the victim system so the adversary can maintain persistence inside the environment — surviving a reboot, a log-off, or simply the passage of time.",
    detect:
      'Host intrusion detection is again the primary example the paper gives; sandboxing a process\'s own privileges (its own example is a Unix "chroot" jail) and antivirus are listed as ways to deny or disrupt the install.',
    attackTactics: [{ name: 'Persistence', id: 'TA0003' }],
    attackCaveat:
      "About as clean a match as this page has — MITRE's own tactic definition, maintaining a foothold, is nearly a paraphrase of the installation phase's own definition above.",
  },
  {
    num: 6,
    name: 'Command and Control',
    icon: 'radio',
    gist: 'The compromised host beacons out to reach its operator.',
    definition:
      'The compromised host beacons outbound to reach an internet-based controller and establish a command-and-control channel. The paper is specific that APT-caliber malware especially favors manual, hands-on-keyboard interaction over fully automated behavior once that channel is open.',
    detect:
      "This phase gets the fullest Courses of Action treatment in the original paper: network intrusion detection watching for beacon patterns, firewall egress rules and network intrusion prevention to deny or disrupt the channel outright, a tarpit to slow a suspected connection down, and redirecting a known-bad C2 domain via DNS to deceive the operator.",
    attackTactics: [{ name: 'Command and Control', id: 'TA0011' }],
    attackCaveat: 'A direct match — same name, same idea, on both sides.',
  },
  {
    num: 7,
    name: 'Actions on Objectives',
    icon: 'crosshair',
    gist: 'Only now can the intruder actually do what it came to do.',
    definition:
      "Only after successfully moving through the first six phases can the intruder act on its actual objective. The paper names data exfiltration — collecting, encrypting, and extracting information — as the typical goal, with violating data integrity or availability as alternate objectives, or simply using the initial foothold as a hop point for further lateral movement deeper into the network.",
    detect:
      "Audit logging is the paper's own primary example at this final stage — by definition, every earlier control has already failed to stop the intrusion. It also lists rate-limiting/quality-of-service controls to degrade the speed of bulk exfiltration, and a honeypot to deceive an adversary who's gotten this far.",
    attackTactics: [
      { name: 'Credential Access', id: 'TA0006' },
      { name: 'Discovery', id: 'TA0007' },
      { name: 'Lateral Movement', id: 'TA0008' },
      { name: 'Collection', id: 'TA0009' },
      { name: 'Exfiltration', id: 'TA0010' },
      { name: 'Impact', id: 'TA0040' },
    ],
    attackCaveat:
      "This is where the granularity gap between the two models is starkest. One broad, final kill-chain phase covers ground ATT&CK spreads across several dedicated Enterprise tactics — the six chips above are the most directly relevant, and Privilege Escalation and Persistence often recur here too as an intruder pivots to a second host. Treat this as a representative sample, not a complete or exhaustive mapping — there is no single tactic to point to.",
  },
];

const totalAttackTactics = ATTACK_TACTIC_ORDER.length;

const sectionH2 = css({ display: 'flex', alignItems: 'center', gap: '0.6rem', fontSize: '1.3rem', fontWeight: 700, mt: '2.5rem', mb: '1rem', pt: '1.5rem', borderTop: '1px solid token(colors.border)' });
const bodyP = css({ color: 'text', lineHeight: 1.8, fontSize: '1rem', mb: '1rem' });

const introAside = css({ bg: 'bgSubtle', border: '1px solid token(colors.border)', borderLeftWidth: '3px', borderLeftColor: 'primary', borderRadius: 'md', p: '1rem 1.25rem', mb: '1rem' });
const introP = css({ color: 'text', lineHeight: 1.7, fontSize: '0.95rem', m: 0 });

const statsLine = css({ display: 'flex', flexWrap: 'wrap', gap: '0.4rem 1.25rem', mb: '1.75rem', fontSize: '0.82rem', color: 'textMuted' });
const statItem = css({ display: 'inline-flex', alignItems: 'center', gap: '0.4rem' });

const crossLinkBox = css({
  display: 'flex',
  alignItems: 'center',
  gap: '0.6rem',
  mb: '1.5rem',
  p: '0.7rem 1rem',
  borderRadius: 'md',
  border: '1px solid token(colors.border)',
  bg: 'bgCard',
  fontSize: '0.9rem',
  color: 'text',
  transition: 'border-color 200ms ease, color 200ms ease',
  _hover: { borderColor: 'primary', color: 'primary', '& .x-arrow': { transform: 'translateX(3px)' } },
});

// The per-phase visual spine — a vertical rail connecting numbered badges,
// closer to a real chain diagram than a plain card list (fitting, for a page
// named after a chain). osi-model.astro used this same idiom once, but was
// later rebuilt to plain bordered cards with no spine (see CLAUDE.md's
// Debugging section); this page's own spine is unaffected by that.
const stack = css({ display: 'flex', flexDirection: 'column', gap: '0.9rem', mt: '1.25rem', position: 'relative', pl: '1.4rem', borderLeft: '2px solid token(colors.border)' });
const phaseRow = `${card()} ${css({ cursor: 'default', position: 'relative' })}`;
const spineDot = css({ position: 'absolute', left: 'calc(-1.4rem - 5px)', top: '1.35rem', w: '8px', h: '8px', borderRadius: 'full', bg: 'primary', boxShadow: '0 0 0 3px token(colors.bg)' });
const phaseHead = css({ display: 'flex', alignItems: 'center', gap: '0.6rem', flexWrap: 'wrap' });
const numBadge = css({
  display: 'inline-flex',
  alignItems: 'center',
  justifyContent: 'center',
  width: '2.1rem',
  height: '2.1rem',
  borderRadius: 'md',
  bg: 'color-mix(in srgb, token(colors.primary) 15%, transparent)',
  color: 'primary',
  fontFamily: 'mono',
  fontWeight: 700,
  fontSize: '0.85rem',
  flexShrink: 0,
});
const phaseIconTile = css({ color: 'primary', flexShrink: 0 });
const phaseName = css({ fontFamily: 'sans', fontWeight: 700, fontSize: '1.08rem', color: 'text' });
const gistText = css({ color: 'textMuted', fontSize: '0.85rem', lineHeight: 1.5, m: 0, flexBasis: '100%' });

const blockLabel = css({
  fontFamily: 'sans',
  fontSize: '0.68rem',
  fontWeight: 700,
  textTransform: 'uppercase',
  letterSpacing: '0.07em',
  color: 'primary',
  mt: '0.9rem',
  mb: '0.3rem',
});
const blockText = css({ color: 'text', fontSize: '0.9rem', lineHeight: 1.7, m: 0 });
const definitionText = css({ color: 'text', fontSize: '0.9rem', lineHeight: 1.7, m: 0, mt: '0.75rem' });
const attackChipRow = css({ display: 'flex', flexWrap: 'wrap', gap: '0.5rem', mt: '0.5rem' });
const attackNote = css({ color: 'textMuted', fontSize: '0.85rem', lineHeight: 1.65, mt: '0.5rem', mb: 0 });
const attackChip = `${toolChip} ${css({ fontSize: '0.78rem', py: '0.32rem', px: '0.6rem' })}`;

const relatedRow = css({ display: 'flex', flexWrap: 'wrap', gap: '0.6rem', mt: '0.85rem' });
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Cyber Kill Chain Reference"
  description="Lockheed Martin's 7-phase Cyber Kill Chain, phase by phase — real definitions, what defenders look for, and how each phase maps (or doesn't) onto MITRE ATT&CK."
>
  <PageHeader
    breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Cyber Kill Chain Reference' }]}
    eyebrow="Frameworks"
    title="Cyber Kill Chain Reference"
    description="Lockheed Martin's seven-phase model of a targeted intrusion — each phase's real definition, what defenders look for, and where it does (and doesn't) line up with MITRE ATT&CK."
  />

  <aside class={introAside}>
    <p class={introP}>
      <Term slug="cyber-kill-chain"><strong class={css({ color: 'text' })}>The Cyber Kill Chain</strong></Term> is
      Lockheed Martin's seven-phase model of how a targeted intrusion unfolds — reconnaissance, weaponization,
      delivery, exploitation, installation, command and control, and actions on objectives — published in a 2011
      paper written in response to the rise of the <Term slug="advanced-persistent-threat">Advanced Persistent
      Threat</Term>. Its central claim: an intrusion is a chain, and breaking any single link anywhere along it stops
      the whole attack. Every phase below states its own real definition from that paper, what Lockheed Martin's own
      detect/deny/disrupt/degrade/deceive/destroy <Term slug="courses-of-action-matrix">Courses of Action
      Matrix</Term> names as the controls that work against it, and — only where a genuine correspondence exists —
      which MITRE ATT&CK tactic covers the same ground.
    </p>
  </aside>

  <div class={statsLine}>
    <span class={statItem}><Icon name="list" size={14} /> 7 phases</span>
    <span class={statItem}><Icon name="calendar" size={14} /> Lockheed Martin, 2011</span>
    <span class={statItem}><Icon name="crosshair" size={14} /> Checked against {totalAttackTactics} MITRE ATT&CK Enterprise tactics</span>
  </div>

  <a href="/attack-map/" class={crossLinkBox}>
    <Icon name="crosshair" size={16} class={css({ color: 'primary', flexShrink: 0 })} />
    <span>See the granular offensive techniques — <strong class={css({ fontWeight: 700 })}>MITRE ATT&CK Coverage Map</strong></span>
    <Icon name="arrow-right" size={14} class={`x-arrow ${css({ ml: 'auto', color: 'textMuted', transition: 'transform 200ms ease', flexShrink: 0 })}`} />
  </a>

  <div class={stack}>
    {KILL_CHAIN_PHASES.map((p) => (
      <article class={phaseRow}>
        <span class={spineDot} />
        <div class={phaseHead}>
          <span class={numBadge}>{p.num}</span>
          <Icon name={p.icon} size={20} class={phaseIconTile} />
          <span class={phaseName}>{p.name}</span>
          <p class={gistText}>{p.gist}</p>
        </div>

        <p class={definitionText}>{p.definition}</p>

        <p class={blockLabel}>What defenders look for</p>
        <p class={blockText}>{p.detect}</p>

        <p class={blockLabel}>Maps to MITRE ATT&CK</p>
        {p.attackTactics.length > 0 && (
          <div class={attackChipRow}>
            {p.attackTactics.map((t) => (
              <a href={`/attack-map/#${tagSlug(t.name)}`} class={attackChip}>
                {t.name} <span class={css({ fontFamily: 'mono', fontSize: '0.7rem', color: 'textMuted' })}>{t.id}</span>
              </a>
            ))}
          </div>
        )}
        <p class={attackNote}>{p.attackCaveat}</p>
      </article>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 class={sectionH2}><Icon name="alert-triangle" size={18} class={css({ color: 'primary' })} /> A linear model, and where that stops fitting</h2>
  <p class={bodyP}>
    The Kill Chain's central metaphor is a straight line: seven links, in that exact order, and breaking any one of
    them stops the whole intrusion. That's a genuinely useful idea — it's the ancestor of a lot of modern detection
    engineering — but it's also the model's biggest limitation once a real intrusion doesn't behave in a straight
    line.
  </p>
  <p class={bodyP}>
    Real intrusions loop, skip phases, and run in parallel. An adversary who reuses infrastructure and tooling across
    many victims doesn't restart Weaponization for each one. Living-off-the-land activity — abusing administrative
    tools already present on a box, like PowerShell or WMI, instead of dropping custom malware — can skip
    Weaponization and Delivery almost entirely, since there's nothing to build or deliver in the first place. An
    insider with legitimate credentials starts past Reconnaissance, Weaponization, Delivery, and Exploitation
    altogether — which is most of what the model was built to interrupt.
  </p>
  <p class={bodyP}>
    MITRE's own FAQ names the same trade-off directly: it describes ATT&CK as working at a more granular level of
    definition than the Cyber Kill Chain, and its own Enterprise tactics are deliberately left unordered, since an
    adversary's own tactical goals shift over the course of an operation instead of marching through seven fixed
    steps — a direct departure from the Kill Chain's fixed sequence. One piece of history shows the same seam:
    MITRE's original PRE-ATT&CK matrix once covered roughly the
    ground of this page's first two-and-a-half phases (reconnaissance through early delivery) as its own separate
    model. In ATT&CK version 8 (October 2020), MITRE folded it directly into the Enterprise matrix as two ordinary,
    still-unordered tactics — Reconnaissance and Resource Development — collapsing what used to be two separate
    frameworks into one continuous one.
  </p>
  <p class={bodyP}>
    Several practitioners have tried to fuse the two directly rather than picking one. The{' '}
    <Term slug="unified-kill-chain">Unified Kill Chain</Term>, published by Paul Pols, stretches the same idea
    across 18 phases spanning the full intrusion lifecycle and explicitly allows phases to repeat, skip, or run out
    of order — closer to how a real, non-linear intrusion actually plays out.
  </p>
  <p class={bodyP}>
    None of this makes the Kill Chain wrong, just coarse. It's still a fine shared vocabulary for a phishing-driven
    external intrusion, and its own Courses of Action Matrix — cited throughout the phases above — is a genuinely
    reusable idea independent of the phase model it was built for. For anything with real lateral movement, an
    insider, or living-off-the-land tradecraft, this site's own <a href="/attack-map/" class={css({ color: 'primary', _hover: { textDecoration: 'underline' } })}>MITRE ATT&CK Coverage Map</a> is the
    finer-grained tool for the job.
  </p>

  <div class={relatedRow}>
    <a href="/attack-map/" class={toolChip}><Icon name="crosshair" size={14} /> MITRE ATT&CK Coverage Map</a>
    <a href="/d3fend/" class={toolChip}><Icon name="shield" size={14} /> MITRE D3FEND Map</a>
    <a href="/threat-actors/" class={toolChip}><Icon name="user" size={14} /> Threat Actor / APT Reference</a>
  </div>

  <ExternalResources resources={EXTERNAL_RESOURCES['kill-chain']} />
</BaseLayout>
